  "rationale": "The two hard system constraints force the architecture: throughput>=32 msps and sys_p99_batch<=0.44us mean 32 results must be produced within 0.44us of a tick, i.e. ~14 cycles. Only the 1-result/cycle families (pipelined, pipelined_m) can do that; iterative and unrolled_k emit one result every several cycles and would queue the burst, blowing the p99 batch bound, so they are excluded. The accuracy constraint (max_abs_err<=2^-12) depends only on data_width/n_iter/guards/rounding, so I bound data_width 14-20 and n_iter 12-18 to bracket the feasible accuracy region without wasting evals on grossly over- or under-accurate corners. The trade-off to map is area (luts_plus_ffs, the objective and selection rule) vs Fmax (needed to meet the batch deadline) vs accuracy_bits: pipelined gives the highest Fmax (safest for the deadline) at higher FF cost, while pipelined_m trades Fmax for lower FFs. Searching both with equal weight lets NSGA-II map the full front between the Fmax-limited and area-limited corners, which is what the hypervolume rewards. I split the budget evenly (0.5/0.5) since both families are plausible front members and the selection rule will pick the cheapest passing design, which could come from either."
